  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain should diminish later tonight into Monday\nwith drier weather anticipated for Tuesday.\n\nThe latest meso-scale model and radar trends continue to offer\na departure of light rain out of much of the area this evening.\nSome lingering patchy light rain may still exists a bit into the\nearly morning hours of Monday.\n\nPatchy fog should develop overnight as skies partially clear. A\ncold front will bring more clouds and light rain showers along\nthe mountains from Boone to Lewisburg by Monday morning. The \nfront should weaken while crossing the Blue Ridge, but an \nisolated shower could briefly form east of Route 29 towards \nMonday evening. Winds should accelerate from the northwest and \nmay become breezy after the frontal passage. Once high pressure \nbuilds across the Appalachian Mountains on Monday night, drier \nweather will resume by Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Wednesday and Thanksgiving Day look increasingly\ncool and wet, and mountain snow showers may arrive by Friday.\n\nAs high pressure wedges against the eastern slopes of the Blue\nRidge by Tuesday night, another coastal low should approach \nfrom the south. This low appears more robust and has more \ndynamical energy available with an upper level trough becoming \nnegatively tilted over the Ohio River Valley. Consequently, it \nis becoming more likely that Wednesday and Thanksgiving Day will\nbe cool and wet. Despite the cold air damming pattern in place,\nthe thermal profile appears largely too warm to support frozen \nprecipitation. One notable change is that the timing for the \narrival of moisture has slowed down by several hours, which will\nmake any initial burst of freezing rain in the highest \nelevations even more unlikely. Thus, rain appears to be the \npredominant precipitation type for this event.\n\nAfter a considerable amount of rainfall and perhaps the same in\nterms of turkey with stuffing and gravy, the coastal low will \ndepart to the northeast by Thursday night. A gusty northwest \nflow behind this low should allow increasing cold air advection \nand upslope snow showers for the mountains of southeast West \nVirginia on Friday. The northwest wind should subside by Friday \nnight. Lingering moisture from the upper level trough may \ntrigger more rain showers into the weekend with some mountain \nsnow showers possible, but this activity appears weak as it will\nnot have a favorable wind pattern with the flow out of the west\nrather than the northwest.",
